// DeadGlobalElimination — WHICH TOP-LEVEL GLOBALS THE PROGRAM CAN STILL NAME (P1.7 slice 3).
//
// A top-level binding with storage costs three separate things, and a binding nothing reads or writes
// pays all three for nothing: an 8-byte `.data` slot, the ops in `__module_init` that build its record
// and store it, and the drop in `__maxon_global_cleanup` that releases it. MEASURED on the base of this
// slice: `var UNUSED = [1, 2, 3]` in a program that never mentions it emitted **3,676 bytes** of code
// against **1,512** for the same program with no global at all — the array runtime, the two synthesized
// functions and the entry stub's two calls, all of it reachable by nothing.
//
// ⭐ **IT DECIDES WHAT TO BUILD. IT DOES NOT REMOVE WHAT WAS BUILT — and that is why it is a MAXON-tier
// derivation rather than a pass beside `DeadFunctionElimination` at the Std tier**, which is where both
// references do the equivalent job. In shv2 all three pieces are produced BEFORE the pipeline runs:
// `ProgramSignatures.declaredGlobals` reserves the slots and `ModuleInit.installManagedGlobalInit`
// synthesizes the two functions, both from the SAME arena, both in `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`. So a
// filter applied to that arena drops all three atomically **by construction** — there is no init run to
// splice out of a lowered body, no `.data` table to re-lay-out, and no ordering between three edits to
// get right. The references have to remove because their `.data` layout is already fixed by the time
// they know; shv2 knows first.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE EVIDENCE IS THE EMITTED `globalAddr` LABEL — NOT A PREDICATE OVER NAMES.** A use of a global
// resolves local → capture → top-level, and that order has now produced a silent wrong answer in three
// consecutive slices every time something re-derived it. So nothing here re-derives it: liveness is read
// off the ops the resolution ALREADY PRODUCED. `Parser.emitGlobalAddr` is the only emitter of a
// `globalAddr` in user code, and it is reached only from `emitGlobalLoad`/`emitGlobalStore` — so the set
// below is built from the very ops that would reference the slot, and the dangerous direction ("an op
// names a slot the layout dropped") is not merely unlikely, it is unrepresentable. A binding that is
// resolved but never emitted merely fails to keep a slot nothing asks for.
//
// ⭐ **AND IF THIS FILE IS EVER WRONG ANYWAY, IT FAILS LOUD — ON EVERY TARGET.** The argument above is an
// argument; this is the mechanism behind it. All three backends resolve a `globalAddr`'s label through the
// ONE lookup `GlobalDataTable.dataSectionOffsetOf`, which PANICS by name on a label the layout does not
// hold (x64 `X64Backend:1804`, arm64 `Arm64Backend:1344-1345`, wasm `StdToWasm:1697`). So an under-walk
// here — a block source this loop cannot see, a `globalAddr` emitted by something new after it runs — is a
// COMPILE-TIME abort naming the global, never a miscompile. MEASURED (P1.7 slice 3 review) by forcing the
// walk to answer "nothing is live" and compiling a program that reads one global: all three targets died
// with `no .data slot is labelled '__data_G'`. That is what makes this pass safe to extend.
//
// ⭐ **BIDIRECTIONALITY IS FREE HERE, AND IT IS THE THREE-OP SPLIT THAT MAKES IT SO.** The spec requires
// that a var only ever STORED to survives — "stores are observable through `globalLoad` or a debugger"
// (`specs/dead-top-level-var-elim.md:32`) — and states liveness as "a `globalLoad` OR a `globalStore`
// … keeps the var alive". shv2 has neither fused op: a read is `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect` and a write
// is `globalAddr` + `storeIndirect`, so both open with the SAME op and there is no second question to
// ask. A rule that had to name both forms could be half-written; this one cannot.
//
// ⚠ **THE ORACLE'S NAMING HEURISTIC IS DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED, and it is a MEASURED bug rather than a
// difference of taste.** `maxon-sharp`'s `DeadFunctionElimination.cs:181-183` decides whether an
// initializer's side effects may be removed with `callee.EndsWith(".create") || callee.EndsWith(".from")`,
// so `let UNUSED = Counter.build()` keeps its `print` when dead while the IDENTICAL body renamed
// `Counter.create()` silently loses it — both compiling clean, with no warning. **Renaming a method must
// not change whether its side effects run.** The conservative behaviour is what the spec actually asks
// for: "the call still runs even when the slot is dead … keeps the call but drops the slot" (:31).
//
// Today shv2 cannot even reach that case — a top-level initializer is a CONSTANT (a call in one is
// E2045), so every op `__module_init` emits for a global is compiler-synthesized allocation with no
// user-visible effect, and dropping the whole run is exactly dropping the slot. The obligation lands the
// day an initializer may call: the call belongs to `ModuleInit.initialRecordOf`, which is where "what
// does this global's record cost to build?" is answered, and it must keep emitting the call while this
// file goes on dropping the slot.
//
// ⚠ **NO REACHABILITY TERM, ON PURPOSE.** The spec says "never read or written by any REACHABLE
// function"; this says "by any function", which keeps strictly MORE globals and is therefore only ever a
// missed prune. Reachability is `DeadFunctionElimination`'s fact, settled at the Std tier after `.data`
// is laid out — too late to decide what to build — and the only reachability available this early is
// `StdlibSource.reachableMaxonFunctionNames`, whose own header records why it may not be widened: it
// walks `call`/`tryCall`/`functionRef`/`asyncCall` edges only, so a function reached through a witness
// table or an `.rdata` slot looks unreachable, and it is "scoped to stdlib source rather than applied to
// every function" for exactly that reason. Letting it decide a `.data` slot would be a SECOND decider of
// reachability, disagreeing with the first in the direction that drops a live global. The refinement is
// worth having when it can be had exactly, and it is unreachable today in any case: no stdlib module
// shv2 loads declares a top-level binding, and the stdlib case the spec exists for
// (`var captured = TraceKeyArray.create()` in `stdlib/Log.maxon`) is a CALL, which shv2 rejects.

// The `.data` labels live code can still name. Keyed by `ByteArray` because that is what both sides
// already hold — the `globalAddr` op's `label` and `TopLevelDecl.dataLabel` are the same bytes, minted
// once by `assignDataLabels` — so asking the question renders nothing and allocates nothing.
export typealias LiveGlobalLabelSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐ **EVERY `.data` LABEL SOME FUNCTION IN THE PROGRAM NAMES.** Called once per compile, on the merged
// Maxon module, BEFORE `installManagedGlobalInit` — and that position is load-bearing twice over. It is
// what lets the answer decide whether the two synthesized functions are built at all, and it is what
// keeps them OUT of the walk: `__module_init` opens every managed global with a `globalAddr` of its own
// slot, so a scan run afterwards would find every global keeping itself alive. Both reference compilers
// meet the same shape in their init store and answer it with a runtime `func.Name == "__module_init"`
// compare; here the fact is expressed by WHICH CODE HAS RUN, which is the same argument `ModuleInit`'s
// header makes about the store's missing decref.
//
// COST: ONE O(ops) walk, allocation-free but for the labels it keeps — and SKIPPED ENTIRELY for a
// program that declares no stored global, which is the whole existing corpus and the entire scale
// ladder. That guard is not a micro-optimization: an unconditional second full-module walk is a
// per-compile TIME cost that allocates nothing, and a cost that allocates nothing is one `scale-test`
// is structurally blind to.
export function liveGlobalLabels(module MaxonModule, storedGlobals StoredGlobalCount) returns LiveGlobalLabelSet
	var live = LiveGlobalLabelSet.create()

	// Not a shortcut past the answer — it IS the answer. With no slot in the program no `globalAddr` can
	// name one, so the walk could only ever build this same empty set.
	if storedGlobals == 0 'noStoredGlobals'
		return live
	end 'noStoredGlobals'

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// Walk `block.opRefs` DIRECTLY rather than `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which allocates a fresh
		// op-index array per function: this runs at the Maxon tier BEFORE any block-rebuilding pass, so
		// `opRefs` holds no orphan ops — the same reason `StdlibSource.markMaxonCallees` and
		// `scanRuntimeUsage` walk it this way. A TERMINATOR can never carry a label (`globalAddr` defines
		// a value and terminators are the returns and branches), so the body ops are the whole of it.
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("liveGlobalLabels: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("liveGlobalLabels: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				noteGlobalLabel(op, live: live)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return live
end 'liveGlobalLabels'

// The `.data` label one `MaxonOp` names, if any. ONE variant carries one — `globalAddr` — and it carries
// it for a read and a write alike, which is the whole of this pass's bidirectionality.
//
// The arms RANGE over the whole union in declaration order, ending at the current last variant, so a
// variant appended past it FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently swallowed — the band-append
// invariant working in the reviewer's favour (MaxonDialect's header), exactly as
// `StdlibSource.markMaxonCalleeEdge` and `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf` rely on it. A new
// variant that names a `.data` label must be added as its own arm.
function noteGlobalLabel(op MaxonOp, live LiveGlobalLabelSet)
	match op 'op'
		// `call` … `branch`: calls, literals, arithmetic, comparisons, branches and returns.
		call to branch then break 'op'
		globalAddr(_, label) then live.insert(label)
		// `loadIndirect` … `witnessDispatch`: the load and the store that FOLLOW a `globalAddr` take the
		// address as a VALUE — the label is on the op above and nowhere else — and no later variant names
		// a data label at all.
		loadIndirect to witnessDispatch then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteGlobalLabel'
